          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_11_equation_6.jpg",
          "content": "(ii)  \\quad \\underset{ H - C = O }{ H }"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.184,
            0.251,
            0.441,
            0.311
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_11_equation_7.jpg",
          "content": "(iii)  \\begin{array}{c}\\mathbb{H} - \\overset{\\mathbb{H}}{\\underset{\\mathbb{H}}{\\overset{\\vert}{\\mathbb{C}}}} - \\overset{\\mathbb{H}}{\\underset{\\mathbb{H}}{\\vert}} - \\overset{\\mathbb{H}}{\\underset{\\mathbb{H}}{\\vert}} - \\overset{\\mathbb{H}}{\\underset{\\mathbb{H}}{\\vert}} \\\\\\end{array}\\begin{array}{c}\\mathbb{H} \\\\\\overset{\\vert}{\\mathbb{C}} - \\overset{\\mathbb{H}}{\\underset{\\vert}{\\vert}} \\\\\\overset{\\vert}{\\underset{\\mathbb{H}}{\\vert}} \\\\\\end{array}\\begin{array}{c}\\mathbb{H} \\\\- \\mathbb{C} \\\\\\end{array}\\begin{array}{c}\\mathbb{H} \\\\\\vert \\\\\\end{array}\\begin{array}{c}\\mathbb{H} \\\\\\vert \\\\\\end{array}\\begin{array}{c}\\mathbb{C} \\\\\\mathbb{H} \\\\\\end{array}\\begin{array}{c}\\mathbb{C} \\\\\\end{array}\\begin{array}{c}\\mathbb{H} \\\\\\end{array}"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.331,
            0.672,
            0.351
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "4.3 CHEMICAL PROPERTIES OF CARBON COMPOUNDS"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.359,
            0.724,
            0.411
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "In this section we will be studying about some of the chemical properties of carbon compounds. Since most of the fuels we use are either carbon or its compounds, we shall first study combustion.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.423,
            0.317,
            0.444
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "4.3.1 Combustion"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.452,
            0.724,
            0.518
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Carbon, in all its allotropic forms, burns in oxygen to give carbon dioxide along with the release of heat and light. Most carbon compounds also release a large amount of heat and light on burning. These are the oxidation reactions that you learnt about in the first Chapter –",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.128,
            0.523,
            0.429,
            0.54
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(i) "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\mathrm{C}+\\mathrm{O}_{2} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{CO}_{2}+\\mathrm{heat}   and light"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.124,
            0.54,
            0.506,
            0.556
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(ii)",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\mathrm{CH_{4}+O_{2}\\rightarrow CO_{2}+H_{2}O+heat and light}"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.119,
            0.556,
            0.569,
            0.576
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(iii) "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\mathrm{CH_{3}CH_{2}OH + O_{2} \\rightarrow CO_{2} + H_{2}O}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "+ heat and light",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.129,
            0.579,
            0.715,
            0.598
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Balance the latter two reactions like you learnt in the first Chapter.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.141,
            0.61,
            0.296,
            0.633
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Activity 4.3"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 17,
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.645,
            0.569,
            0.662
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "CAUTION: This Activity needs the teacher’s assistance.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 18,
          "bbox": [
            0.648,
            0.611,
            0.802,
            0.633
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Activity 4.4"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 19,
          "bbox": [
            0.109,
            0.664,
            0.571,
            0.708
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Take some carbon compounds (naphthalene, camphor, alcohol) one by one on a spatula and burn them.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 20,
          "bbox": [
            0.109,
            0.709,
            0.571,
            0.74
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Observe the nature of the flame and note whether smoke is produced.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 21,
          "bbox": [
            0.629,
            0.647,
            0.901,
            0.706
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Light a bunsen burner and adjust the air hole at the base to get different types of flames/presence of smoke.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 22,
          "bbox": [
            0.629,
            0.706,
            0.899,
            0.735
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "When do you get a yellow, sooty flame?",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 23,
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.742,
            0.571,
            0.773
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Place a metal plate above the flame. Is there a deposition on the plate in case of any of the compounds?",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 24,
          "bbox": [
            0.629,
            0.735,
            0.9,
            0.765
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "When do you get a blue flame?",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 25,
          "bbox": [
            0.099,
            0.788,
            0.724,
            0.884
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Saturated hydrocarbons will generally give a clean flame while unsaturated carbon compounds will give a yellow flame with lots of black smoke. This results in a sooty deposit on the metal plate in Activity 4.3. However, limiting the supply of air results in incomplete combustion of even saturated hydrocarbons giving a sooty flame. The gas/kerosene stove used at home has inlets for air so that a sufficiently oxygen-rich",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "index": 26,
          "bbox": [
            0.1,
            0.915,
            0.304,
            0.93
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Carbon and its Compounds"
            }
          ]
        },
